std.flag — Command-line flag parsing
====================================

A safe, idiomatic command-line flag parser inspired by Go's `flag`
package, with two ergonomic upgrades: every flag has both a short
(`-v`) and a long (`--verbose`) name, and combined short flags
(`-abc` for three booleans) are supported. Parse errors come back
as `Result[ParsedArgs, Error]` instead of panicking, so callers can
print a usage message and exit cleanly.

Usage:

```
var p = new_parser("myapp", "does useful things")

val opt_v = p.bool_flag("v", "verbose", "enable verbose output")
val opt_p = p.int_flag("p", "port", 8080, "server port")
val opt_o = p.str_flag("o", "output", "", "output file")

val r = p.parse(args)

if is_err(r) then ...

val parsed = unwrap(r)
val verbose = parsed.get_bool(opt_v)
val port = parsed.get_int(opt_p)
val output = parsed.get_str(opt_o)
val files = parsed.args()
```

Each `*_flag` registration returns an `int` index handle that the
caller stashes in a `val`; later, `get_bool` / `get_int` / `get_str`
on the `ParsedArgs` retrieve the value at that index. This avoids
generics, pointers, and dynamic dispatch entirely — flag access is
plain array indexing.

Supported syntax for a single argv entry:

| Form | Example | Notes |
|------|---------|-------|
| Short bool | `-v` | Sets the bool to `true` |
| Combined short bools | `-abc` | Each character sets one bool |
| Short with value (separate) | `-p 8080` | Next argv entry is the value |
| Short with value (attached) | `-p8080`, `-ofile.txt` | Same arg holds the value |
| Long bool | `--verbose` | |
| Long with value (`=`) | `--port=8080` | |
| Long with value (separate) | `--port 8080` | Next argv entry is the value |
| End-of-flags | `--` | Everything after is positional |
| Bare dash | `-` | Treated as a positional (stdin convention) |

Dependencies: `std.errors`, `std.result`, `std.strconv` (for
`parse_i64`), `std.strings` (for `has_prefix`), `std.builder` (for
slicing argv tokens).

    module std.flag

    import std.debug.{assert}
    import std.errors.{Error, new_error}
    import std.result.{Result, Ok, Err, is_ok, is_err, unwrap}
    import std.strconv.{parse_i64}
    import std.strings.{has_prefix}
    import std.builder.{StrBuilder, new_builder_cap}


Constants
---------

Flag-type tags stored in `FlagDesc.flag_type`. The fixed `MAX_FLAGS`
of 32 is a deliberate choice — it caps the per-parser table at a
modest size and keeps the in-memory descriptor table cache-friendly.

    val FLAG_BOOL = 0
    val FLAG_INT  = 1
    val FLAG_STR  = 2

    val MAX_FLAGS = 32


Flag descriptor
---------------

One descriptor per registered flag, stored in the parser's fixed
`flags` array.

    struct FlagDesc
        short_name: string
        long_name: string
        help_text: string
        flag_type: int
        default_int: int
        default_str: string


Parsed result
-------------

`ParsedArgs` is what `parse()` returns on success. It holds the
parsed flag values (one per registered flag, indexed by the handle
returned during registration) and the positional arguments collected
in declaration order.

    struct ParsedArgs
        bool_vals: [32]bool
        int_vals: [32]int
        str_vals: [32]string
        positional: []string
        positional_count: int

    ParsedArgs.get_bool(idx: int) -> bool = self.bool_vals[idx]

    ParsedArgs.get_int(idx: int) -> int = self.int_vals[idx]

    ParsedArgs.get_str(idx: int) -> string = self.str_vals[idx]

    ParsedArgs.args() -> []string = self.positional

    ParsedArgs.arg_count() -> int = self.positional_count


Parser
------

The parser owns the flag table and the program metadata used by the
help generator.

    struct Parser
        name: string
        description: string
        flags: [32]FlagDesc
        count: int

    new_parser(name: string, description: string) -> Parser
        var p: Parser
        p.name = name
        p.description = description
        p.count = 0
        return p


### Flag registration

Each registration appends one entry to the parser's flag table and
returns its index. Callers must keep that index — it's the handle
used to retrieve the parsed value later.

    Parser.bool_flag(short_name: string, long_name: string, help_text: string) -> int
        val idx = self.count
        self.flags[idx] = FlagDesc(short_name, long_name, help_text, FLAG_BOOL, 0, "")
        self.count++
        return idx

    Parser.int_flag(short_name: string, long_name: string, default_val: int, help_text: string) -> int
        val idx = self.count
        self.flags[idx] = FlagDesc(short_name, long_name, help_text, FLAG_INT, default_val, "")
        self.count++
        return idx

    Parser.str_flag(short_name: string, long_name: string, default_val: string, help_text: string) -> int
        val idx = self.count
        self.flags[idx] = FlagDesc(short_name, long_name, help_text, FLAG_STR, 0, default_val)
        self.count++
        return idx


### `find_flag` — name → index lookup

Linear scan of the flag table; returns the index, or `-1` if no flag
matches. Linear is fine here: the table is at most 32 entries and
this only runs once per token at parse time.

    Parser.find_flag(name: string) -> int
        for i in 0..<self.count
            if self.flags[i].short_name == name then return i
            if self.flags[i].long_name == name then return i
        return -1


### `parse` — walk argv and produce `ParsedArgs`

`parse(argv)` consumes a list of argument strings (typically
`argv[1:]` after the program name) and returns
`Result[ParsedArgs, Error]`. The state machine handles five branch
classes per token: positional, bare `-`, end-of-flags `--`, long
flag, short flag(s). Each long/short flag may consume a following
token as its value if needed.

    Parser.parse(argv: []string) -> Result[ParsedArgs, Error]
        var result: ParsedArgs

Seed every registered flag with its declared default before parsing
begins. Bool flags default to `false` (already zero-initialized);
ints and strings get the per-flag default supplied at registration.

        for i in 0..<self.count
            val f = self.flags[i]

            if f.flag_type == FLAG_INT
                result.int_vals[i] = f.default_int
            else if f.flag_type == FLAG_STR
                result.str_vals[i] = f.default_str

Allocate a positional buffer the size of `argv`. The actual count is
tracked separately and may be smaller than the buffer.

        val pos = (new [len(argv)]string)[:]
        var pos_count = 0

        var i = 0
        val argc = len(argv)
        var flags_done = false

        while i < argc
            val arg = argv[i]
            val alen = len(arg)

A token that doesn't start with `-` (or anything once `--` has been
seen, or empty) is positional.

            if flags_done || alen == 0 || arg[0] != byte('-')
                pos[pos_count] = arg
                pos_count++
                i++
            else if alen == 1

A bare `-` is the conventional "use stdin/stdout" marker — keep it
as a positional.

                pos[pos_count] = arg
                pos_count++
                i++
            else if alen == 2 && arg[1] == byte('-')

`--` is the end-of-flags marker. Switch to positional-only mode for
all remaining tokens.

                flags_done = true
                i++
            else if arg[1] == byte('-')

**Long flag** of the form `--name`, `--name=value`, or `--name value`.
Walk for an `=` to split the name and inline value.

                var name = ""
                var value = ""
                var has_eq = false
                var j = 2

                while j < alen
                    if arg[j] == byte('=')
                        has_eq = true

The name is the substring between `--` and `=`; the value is
everything after `=`.

                        var nb = new_builder_cap(j - 2)
                        for k in 2..<j
                            nb.write_byte(byte(arg[k]))
                        name = nb.to_str()

                        var vb = new_builder_cap(alen - j - 1)
                        for k in (j + 1)..<alen
                            vb.write_byte(byte(arg[k]))
                        value = vb.to_str()

                        j = alen
                    else
                        j++

If no `=` was found, the entire post-`--` substring is the name.

                if !has_eq
                    var nb = new_builder_cap(alen - 2)
                    for k in 2..<alen
                        nb.write_byte(byte(arg[k]))
                    name = nb.to_str()

                val idx = self.find_flag(name)

                if idx < 0 then return Err(new_error(s"unknown flag: --$name"))

                val f = self.flags[idx]

                if f.flag_type == FLAG_BOOL
                    result.bool_vals[idx] = true
                    i++
                else if has_eq
                    if f.flag_type == FLAG_INT
                        val rv = parse_i64(value)

                        if is_err(rv) then return Err(new_error(s"invalid value for --$name: $value"))

                        result.int_vals[idx] = int(unwrap(rv))
                    else
                        result.str_vals[idx] = value
                    i++
                else

The flag wants a value but didn't carry an `=` — pull the next argv
entry. Error out if there isn't one.

                    i++

                    if i >= argc then return Err(new_error(s"flag --$name requires a value"))

                    val next = argv[i]

                    if f.flag_type == FLAG_INT
                        val rv = parse_i64(next)

                        if is_err(rv) then return Err(new_error(s"invalid value for --$name: $next"))

                        result.int_vals[idx] = int(unwrap(rv))
                    else
                        result.str_vals[idx] = next
                    i++
            else

**Short flag(s)** of the form `-v`, `-abc`, `-p 8080`, or `-p8080`.
The inner loop walks character-by-character so a single argv token
can fold multiple boolean flags together. As soon as a value-taking
flag appears, the remainder of the token (or the next token) is its
value and the inner loop exits.

                j = 1

                while j < alen
                    var sb = new_builder_cap(1)
                    sb.write_byte(byte(arg[j]))
                    val ch = sb.to_str()
                    val idx = self.find_flag(ch)

                    if idx < 0 then return Err(new_error(s"unknown flag: -$ch"))

                    val f = self.flags[idx]

                    if f.flag_type == FLAG_BOOL
                        result.bool_vals[idx] = true
                        j++
                    else

A value-taking short flag has two cases: characters remain in this
token (so `-p8080` and `-ofile.txt` work), or it consumes the next
argv token.

                        if j + 1 < alen
                            var vb = new_builder_cap(alen - j - 1)
                            for k in (j + 1)..<alen
                                vb.write_byte(byte(arg[k]))
                            val value = vb.to_str()

                            if f.flag_type == FLAG_INT
                                val rv = parse_i64(value)

                                if is_err(rv) then return Err(new_error(s"invalid value for -$ch: $value"))

                                result.int_vals[idx] = int(unwrap(rv))
                            else
                                result.str_vals[idx] = value

                            j = alen
                        else
                            i++

                            if i >= argc then return Err(new_error(s"flag -$ch requires a value"))

                            val next = argv[i]

                            if f.flag_type == FLAG_INT
                                val rv = parse_i64(next)

                                if is_err(rv) then return Err(new_error(s"invalid value for -$ch: $next"))

                                result.int_vals[idx] = int(unwrap(rv))
                            else
                                result.str_vals[idx] = next

                            j = alen

                i++

        result.positional = pos
        result.positional_count = pos_count
        return Ok(result)


### `help` — generate a usage block

Builds a multi-line help string with the program name, optional
description, and one line per registered flag (short + long names,
type hint, help text, and default value where meaningful). Use this
to print on `--help` or on parse error.

    Parser.help() -> string
        var b = new_builder_cap(256)
        b.write("Usage: ")
        b.write(self.name)
        b.write(" [options] [arguments]\n")

        if len(self.description) > 0
            b.write(self.description)
            b.write("\n")

        b.write("\nOptions:\n")

        for i in 0..<self.count
            val f = self.flags[i]
            b.write("  -")
            b.write(f.short_name)
            b.write(", --")
            b.write(f.long_name)

            if f.flag_type == FLAG_INT
                b.write(" <int>")
            else if f.flag_type == FLAG_STR
                b.write(" <str>")

            b.write("\t")
            b.write(f.help_text)

            if f.flag_type == FLAG_INT
                b.write(s" (default: ${f.default_int})")
            else if f.flag_type == FLAG_STR && len(f.default_str) > 0
                b.write(s" (default: ${f.default_str})")

            b.write("\n")

        return b.to_str()


Tests
=====

### Bool flags

    #test
    test_bool_short() -> unit
        var p = new_parser("test", "")
        val v = p.bool_flag("v", "verbose", "verbose")
        val argv: [1]string = ["-v"]
        val r = p.parse(argv[:])
        assert(is_ok(r), "parses")
        assert(unwrap(r).get_bool(v), "verbose set")

    #test
    test_bool_long() -> unit
        var p = new_parser("test", "")
        val v = p.bool_flag("v", "verbose", "verbose")
        val argv: [1]string = ["--verbose"]
        val r = p.parse(argv[:])
        assert(is_ok(r), "parses")
        assert(unwrap(r).get_bool(v), "verbose set")

    #test
    test_bool_default_false() -> unit
        var p = new_parser("test", "")
        val v = p.bool_flag("v", "verbose", "verbose")
        val argv: [0]string = []
        val r = p.parse(argv[:])
        assert(is_ok(r), "parses")
        assert(!unwrap(r).get_bool(v), "default false")

    #test
    test_combined_short_bools() -> unit
        var p = new_parser("test", "")
        val a = p.bool_flag("a", "all", "all")
        val l = p.bool_flag("l", "long_flag", "long")
        val h = p.bool_flag("h", "human", "human")
        val argv: [1]string = ["-alh"]
        val r = p.parse(argv[:])
        assert(is_ok(r), "parses")
        val parsed = unwrap(r)
        assert(parsed.get_bool(a), "a set")
        assert(parsed.get_bool(l), "l set")
        assert(parsed.get_bool(h), "h set")


### Int flags

    #test
    test_int_short_space() -> unit
        var p = new_parser("test", "")
        val port = p.int_flag("p", "port", 8080, "port")
        val argv: [2]string = ["-p", "3000"]
        val r = p.parse(argv[:])
        assert(is_ok(r), "parses")
        assert(unwrap(r).get_int(port) == 3000, "port 3000")

    #test
    test_int_long_equals() -> unit
        var p = new_parser("test", "")
        val port = p.int_flag("p", "port", 8080, "port")
        val argv: [1]string = ["--port=3000"]
        val r = p.parse(argv[:])
        assert(is_ok(r), "parses")
        assert(unwrap(r).get_int(port) == 3000, "port 3000")

    #test
    test_int_long_space() -> unit
        var p = new_parser("test", "")
        val port = p.int_flag("p", "port", 8080, "port")
        val argv: [2]string = ["--port", "3000"]
        val r = p.parse(argv[:])
        assert(is_ok(r), "parses")
        assert(unwrap(r).get_int(port) == 3000, "port 3000")

    #test
    test_int_default() -> unit
        var p = new_parser("test", "")
        val port = p.int_flag("p", "port", 8080, "port")
        val argv: [0]string = []
        val r = p.parse(argv[:])
        assert(is_ok(r), "parses")
        assert(unwrap(r).get_int(port) == 8080, "default 8080")


### String flags

    #test
    test_str_short() -> unit
        var p = new_parser("test", "")
        val out = p.str_flag("o", "output", "", "output")
        val argv: [2]string = ["-o", "file.txt"]
        val r = p.parse(argv[:])
        assert(is_ok(r), "parses")
        assert(unwrap(r).get_str(out) == "file.txt", "file.txt")

    #test
    test_str_long_equals() -> unit
        var p = new_parser("test", "")
        val out = p.str_flag("o", "output", "", "output")
        val argv: [1]string = ["--output=file.txt"]
        val r = p.parse(argv[:])
        assert(is_ok(r), "parses")
        assert(unwrap(r).get_str(out) == "file.txt", "file.txt")

    #test
    test_str_attached_short() -> unit
        var p = new_parser("test", "")
        val out = p.str_flag("o", "output", "", "output")
        val argv: [1]string = ["-ofile.txt"]
        val r = p.parse(argv[:])
        assert(is_ok(r), "parses")
        assert(unwrap(r).get_str(out) == "file.txt", "attached")


### Positional arguments

    #test
    test_positional_only() -> unit
        var p = new_parser("test", "")
        val argv: [2]string = ["foo", "bar"]
        val r = p.parse(argv[:])
        assert(is_ok(r), "parses")
        val parsed = unwrap(r)
        assert(parsed.arg_count() == 2, "2 args")
        assert(parsed.args()[0] == "foo", "foo")
        assert(parsed.args()[1] == "bar", "bar")

    #test
    test_mixed_flags_and_positional() -> unit
        var p = new_parser("test", "")
        val v = p.bool_flag("v", "verbose", "verbose")
        val argv: [3]string = ["-v", "foo", "bar"]
        val r = p.parse(argv[:])
        assert(is_ok(r), "parses")
        val parsed = unwrap(r)
        assert(parsed.get_bool(v), "verbose")
        assert(parsed.arg_count() == 2, "2 positional")
        assert(parsed.args()[0] == "foo", "foo")

    #test
    test_double_dash_stops_flags() -> unit
        var p = new_parser("test", "")
        val v = p.bool_flag("v", "verbose", "verbose")
        val argv: [3]string = ["--", "-v", "foo"]
        val r = p.parse(argv[:])
        assert(is_ok(r), "parses")
        val parsed = unwrap(r)
        assert(!parsed.get_bool(v), "v not set")
        assert(parsed.arg_count() == 2, "2 positional")
        assert(parsed.args()[0] == "-v", "-v as positional")


### Error cases

    #test
    test_unknown_short() -> unit
        var p = new_parser("test", "")
        val argv: [1]string = ["-x"]
        val r = p.parse(argv[:])
        assert(is_err(r), "unknown flag")

    #test
    test_unknown_long() -> unit
        var p = new_parser("test", "")
        val argv: [1]string = ["--unknown"]
        val r = p.parse(argv[:])
        assert(is_err(r), "unknown flag")

    #test
    test_missing_value() -> unit
        var p = new_parser("test", "")
        val _ = p.str_flag("o", "output", "", "output")
        val argv: [1]string = ["-o"]
        val r = p.parse(argv[:])
        assert(is_err(r), "missing value")

    #test
    test_invalid_int() -> unit
        var p = new_parser("test", "")
        val _ = p.int_flag("p", "port", 0, "port")
        val argv: [2]string = ["-p", "abc"]
        val r = p.parse(argv[:])
        assert(is_err(r), "invalid int")


### Help generation

    #test
    test_help_contains_flags() -> unit
        var p = new_parser("myapp", "a tool")
        val _ = p.bool_flag("v", "verbose", "be verbose")
        val _ = p.int_flag("p", "port", 8080, "listen port")
        val h = p.help()
        assert(len(h) > 0, "non-empty help")


Design notes
------------

**Index handles, no generics.** Flag registration returns an `int`
index. The caller stores it in a `val` and uses it to retrieve the
parsed value. This avoids generics, pointers, and dynamic dispatch
— flag access becomes plain array indexing on a fixed-size table.
A future generics-aware redesign could expose typed handles, but
the index approach has been comfortable in practice.

**`Result` on error.** `parse` returns `Result[ParsedArgs, Error]`
instead of panicking, so a bad command line surfaces as a normal
return value. Callers `match` it (or use `?` to propagate) and can
print a nice usage message before exiting with a non-zero code.

**Combined short flags with embedded values.** `-abc` sets all three
booleans. If a non-bool flag appears in a combined group, the rest
of the same argv token becomes its value: `-p8080` sets port to 8080
without needing a separate token. This matches the long-standing
Unix convention that drives every well-known CLI tool.

**Fixed-capacity flag table.** 32 flags maximum per parser. That's
enough for any reasonable CLI tool (compare: `git status` exposes
~25 options) and lets the table live entirely on the stack —
zero heap allocation per registration.
